# One finding, one line, and it stays as narrow as the format allows.
#
# gitleaks matches an entry by FINGERPRINT — commit : file : rule : line — so an entry excuses
# exactly one occurrence in one commit and nothing else. That is the whole reason this file is used
# here instead of an allowlist in a `.gitleaks.toml`: a rule- or path-scoped allowlist keeps
# excusing every future line that matches its shape, silently, and a scanner taught to ignore a
# file's shape no longer guards that file. This project has already been bitten by the general
# version of that — a calibration probe copied from a vendor's documentation was allowlisted by the
# scanner's own defaults, so the probe proved nothing.
#
# What is being excused: `tests/e2e/apps_test.go` needed a string SHAPED like an API key, to prove
# that a key which was never issued is rejected exactly as a bad session token is. The scheme is
# deliberately designed to be unmistakable in a repository or a log (see `auth.APIKeyScheme`), which
# is precisely why a plausible body behind it reads as a real key. There has never been a key with
# that value: the server mints the whole token, and nothing outside it can produce one.
#
# The working tree no longer contains it — the literal is assembled from parts at the point of use,
# so a future commit cannot reintroduce the shape without also reintroducing the reasoning. This
# entry exists only because 592b95f is already pushed and history is not rewritten to hide a string
# that was never a secret.
#
# Calibrated 2026-09-05, both directions: with this line, `gitleaks detect` reports 0 findings and
# exits 0; with the same line pointing one row lower (…:774), the finding comes back. So the file
# is suppressing this occurrence and not the rule, not the file, and not the scan.
